## Why this body matches the binding

- `does`: "Run the declared halve or double command, as the step names the
  operation" — `helpers.CommandForOperation` maps the `operation` capture
  (`halves`/`doubles`) onto the CLI document's `halve`/`double` commands, and
  nothing else chooses the command.
- "as a process with the step's whole number" — `RunCalculator` runs
  `go run ./services/calc <command> [--exact] VALUE` with `cmd.Dir` set to the
  host root, exactly the invocation form the CLI document declares. The `number`
  capture is passed through as the VALUE argument as captured, so positive, zero
  and negative numbers all reach the command unaltered; the body neither parses
  nor rewrites it.
- "passing --exact to halve when the step says exactly" — the `exact` capture is
  the only thing that adds `--exact`, and only on `halve`, since the CLI document
  declares the flag on `halve` alone. An empty capture adds no flag.
- "and keep its stdout as the output" — stdout goes to `State.Output`, with only
  the one trailing line terminator removed that the CLI document says each
  command's stdout ends with. No halving, doubling, rounding or decimal
  formatting happens anywhere in the body or the helpers: the printed value is
  carried, never computed, so a later Then observes the product's own answer.
- The inherited environment is kept (`cmd.Env` is left nil), preserving the Go
  build cache and temporary-directory settings of the surrounding run.
- A failed or wedged run returns an error naming the full command, the directory
  and the command's stderr, so a missing or unbuilt target reports itself plainly
  rather than leaving `State.Output` silently empty for the Then.

